The first statement incorrectly passes a boolean value (result of the comparison) to `bootm_process_cmdline_env()`, which should instead receive a flag indicating whether to perform string substitution. This leads to the second statement never evaluating to true, preventing the execution of the substitution logic in `process_subst()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
int flags = 0;
if (images->os.os == IH_OS_LINUX)
    flags = BOOTM_CL_ALL;
ret = bootm_process_cmdline_env(flags);
```
Reason:
These statements correctly initialize a `flags` variable and set it to `BOOTM_CL_ALL` if the OS is Linux. This ensures that the correct flag is passed to `bootm_process_cmdline_env()`, allowing the condition `if (flags & BOOTM_CL_SUBST)` to evaluate to true, thus enabling the string substitution logic to execute properly.

